          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "**Please contact a doctor or urgent care today.** Burning when you pass urine, a temperature, and back pain together can point to an infection that has reached the kidney. A risk tool rated your situation \"routine,\" but that rating came from a fixed rule table, and none of its emergency patterns matched. That is not a clearance. The urinary tract infection reference says fever with flank or back pain needs prompt assessment (*Urinary tract infection*).\n\n**Call emergency services now** if you develop any of these, which the sepsis guidance lists as signs of a life-threatening response to infection (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*): confusion, unusual drowsiness, or slurred speech; fast or difficult breathing, a racing heartbeat, or dizziness when standing; not passing urine for many hours; shaking chills, or a very high or very low temperature; skin that looks mottled, very pale, or blue; or feeling much more unwell than ever before. Also seek urgent help if you are vomiting and cannot keep fluids down, or if you are pregnant or have only one kidney or a kidney transplant (*Urinary tract infection*).\n\n**On treatment:** Treatment is usually an antibiotic chosen by a clinician, guided by local resistance patterns. Fluids and symptom relief support treatment but do not replace it. Symptoms that have not improved within 48 hours of treatment need reassessment. The guidance I could find does not cover kidney infection specifically. The uncomplicated urinary tract infection guideline applies only to bladder infections in non-pregnant adults and excludes suspected kidney involvement, so I can't summarise its antibiotic advice for your situation. I also can't give specific drugs or doses.\n\n**Where sources differ:** The two sources frame urine culture (a lab test to identify the germ) differently. One says culture is generally reserved for uncertain, complicated, or recurrent cases, or when a first treatment has not worked (*Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*). The other says culture is recommended when kidney involvement is suspected (*Urinary tract infection*). A suspected kidney infection would likely fall under the first source's \"complicated\" category, so this may be a difference in emphasis rather than a conflict, but the sources do not settle it. A clinician will decide whether a culture is needed.\n\n**How strong the evidence is:** The material I found gives no evidence grades or treatment durations for kidney infection, so I can't say how strong the evidence behind this advice is.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
